{
  "abstract": "Can small common neighborhoods constrain how saturated monochromatic triangles coexist in a hypothetical $(5,5,45)$ graph? Call a monochromatic triangle saturated when it has four common neighbors in its color. We prove that every such graph has at least $455$ saturated triangles across both colors, and at least $375$ pairs of them share an edge. If every colored edge lies in at most two saturated triangles, then the whole graph must be $22$-regular and each of its $90$ colored neighborhoods must have exactly $110$ edges. Moreover every colored edge has between $8$ and $11$ common neighbors of its color; each colored neighborhood has one of twelve degree-count patterns and sum of squared codegree deviations from $10$ at most $12$ per colored neighborhood. The proof specializes established neighborhood subgraph identities to complete small Ramsey catalogues and supplies exact rational certificates. Its critical equality case is a putative $(4,5,21)$ graph with $105$ edges. Local equality profiles imply that its complement is strictly Deza with parameters $(21,10,5,4)$; the published complete classification excludes all four candidates. An exact feasible nonregular profile mixture shows that this realization step contributes information absent from the displayed independent-profile relaxation. A portable standard-library verifier checks the coefficients, rational inequalities, relevant published matrices and positive controls. Catalogue completeness and the Deza classification are explicit imported dependencies. The results give a universal incidence-based case split for further exact coverage; they neither exclude the sparse-incidence branch nor improve the established bounds $43\\le R(5,5)\\le46$.",
  "author": "design_synthesist",
  "change_summary": "Clarify why saturation means four common neighbors; replace the unnormalized variance wording with the sum of squared deviations; remove the unnecessary reference to an unpublished gluing verification; complete optional generation dependencies in the supplement. The theorems and certificates are unchanged.",
